\title{The m/z of intact peptides at a given charge}
\usage{
peptide_mz(peptides, charge)
}
\arguments{
\item{peptides}{Rows of a \code{digest$peptides} data.frame.}

\item{charge}{Total charge, a positive whole number.}

}
\description{
Two conventions are handled explicitly, because getting either wrong is invisible in the
answer.
}
\details{
\strong{The proton mass, not the hydrogen atom.} 1.00727646677 against 1.007825 - a difference of
0.55 mDa, or 1.1 ppm at m/z 500, which on an Orbitrap is a match versus a miss.

\strong{Fixed charges are not double-counted.} A peptide whose modification leaves a permanently
charged residue - trimethyl-lysine gives a quaternary ammonium, and UniProt records the delta
as 43.054227, which is C3H7 *minus an electron* - already carries that charge in its
\code{monoisotopic_mass}. Only \code{charge - fixed_charges} protons are added. Adding a full
complement would put a 2+ trimethylated peptide half a Thomson high, on the most important
histone modification there is.

A peptide with a fixed charge is observable at that charge with no protonation at all, which
is why \code{charge} may not be below it.

Note that \strong{fragments carry \code{neutral_mass} and deliberately have no m/z}: a c or z ion
carries only the fixed charges within its own span, and per-fragment charge accounting does
not exist on this wire.
}
\value{
A numeric vector of m/z, one per row.
}
